The Workers in the Vineyard
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who owned a vineyard. He went out early in the morning to hire people to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to give them the normal day's wage. Then he sent them to his vineyard.
About nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw other people standing in the marketplace and doing nothing.
He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard. And I will pay you what is right.'
So they walked. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and found other people standing there. He asked them, 'Why do you sit here all day long and don't do anything at all?'
They said to them, 'Because no one has been sent to us.' He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard.'
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the supervisor of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with those who were hired last. Then finish with those who were hired first."
The workers who finished up around five o'clock came. Each one of them took the normal day's wage.
When the first workers came, they expected to receive more. However, each of them also usually received the day's wage.
As soon as they took possession, they started complaining about the owner.
They said: "These people who were last hired only worked for an hour. You paid them the same as us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The owner answered one of them. He said: "My friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the standard wage?"
